  "implementation": [
    "SQLite.TriggerRaise specializes certified RAISE(ABORT, expression) nodes only in trigger-program context. It traverses expressions without executing branches or messages. Scalar subquery SQL remains unchanged and specializes only when compiled in its actual lexical scope.",
    "The normal scalar evaluator prepares names and executes reached message expressions lazily. The error message uses the existing SQLite value-to-text conversion, NULL maps to an empty message, and its first NUL terminates the exposed message. No alternate oracle evaluator or target result substitution is used.",
    "Program WHEN/read preparation recognizes explicit trigger frames. Body binding specializes before name resolution. Actual runtime depth marks live program statements. The private executable function is rejected by the public token matcher; outside RAISE retains its normal diagnostic.",
    "ABORT propagates as an ordinary execution error, so failed outer row loops and pending body writes are unpublished in the current failure-closing file modes. This does not solve same-connection post-error counter retention, IGNORE/FAIL/ROLLBACK control flow or arbitrary deferred private-function binding."
  ],
  "reference_protocol": "Complete stdout/exit/first-error and independently observed persisted catalog/table/integrity contents compare with pinned SQLite 3.53.4 in rollback and WAL modes. Both drivers stop at the failing SQL statement and close the connection. Twelve generated ABORT inputs have a new final-checkpoint seed; all prior suites are rerun with fresh inputs. A separate native continuation diagnostic deliberately omits -bail and is labelled independently. No skips, result rewriting or global 100% claim is used.",
  "crash_scope": "SIGKILL timing samples during a multi-table BEFORE UPDATE that raises ABORT on the final source row after pending body writes. Native reference failure and complete old-state persistence are required. Public Lean recovery precedes independent SQLite catalog/row/integrity checks. This failure-closing protocol does not evaluate post-error connection counters or every syscall/power-loss outcome.",
  "primary_source_basis": "Pinned expr.c RAISE code generation and lazy message evaluation, vdbe.c OP_Halt message conversion and ABORT handling. Native controls determine NULL/NUL/numeric/BLOB/Unicode text and view/trigger context behavior.",
  "proof_scope": "Retained Core/file/physical and signed-counter contracts compile. New audited component laws cover disabled contextual specialization and unchanged scalar-subquery source text. A new executable group checks actual dynamic error text, failed pending writes and an unreached conditional ABORT. File-mode comparisons do not imply same-connection error-state equivalence.",
  "limitations": [
    "Same-connection post-error counter/error-state retention remains open: the existing Except String interface loses the native retained total_changes count after an aborting body write. IGNORE/FAIL/ROLLBACK, INSTEAD OF/temp triggers, triggered UPSERT, trigger-aware ALTER, writable CTEs, deferred private-function declaration binding and wider query/error/FK combinations remain work.",
    "Eight of 88 named probes still fail: WITHOUT ROWID, composite primary keys and partial/expression indexes, both modes. These probes do not define global coverage.",
    "UTF-16 writes/setters, native-key/materialization limits, pending new physical allocations and earlier SQL/storage limitations remain. The finite process-kill samples do not establish every syscall/power-loss schedule."
  ],
